\begin{abstract}
	This work focuses on the credit assignment problem in cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL). Sharing the global advantage among agents often leads to suboptimal policy updates as it fails to account for the distinct contributions of agents. Although numerous methods consider global or individual contributions for credit assignment, a detailed analysis at the coalition level remains lacking in many approaches. This work analyzes the over-updating problem during multi-agent policy updates from a coalition-level perspective. To address this issue, we propose a credit assignment method called Coalitional Rational Advantage Decomposition (CORA). CORA evaluates coalitional advantages via marginal contributions from all possible coalitions and decomposes advantages using the core solution from cooperative game theory, ensuring coalitional rationality. To reduce computational overhead, CORA employs random coalition sampling. Experiments on matrix games, differential games, and multi-agent collaboration benchmarks demonstrate that CORA outperforms strong baselines, particularly in tasks with multiple local optima. These findings highlight the importance of coalition-aware credit assignment for improving MARL performance.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{Sec:Introduction}
Cooperative Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) aims to train a group of agents to jointly maximize a shared objective in a common environment \cite{panait2005cooperative}. Such a paradigm has shown great potential in a wide range of applications \cite{hu2022marllib}, including autonomous driving platoons \cite{shalev2016safe}, multi-robot systems \cite{busoniu2008comprehensive}, and large-scale network control \cite{Ma2024efficient}. A key challenge in MARL is how to effectively coordinate decentralized agents so that they can learn global strategies that maximize the global return while still relying on local observations during execution \cite{oliehoek2008optimal,lowe2017multi}.

Recent advances in policy-gradient-based algorithms have significantly improved stability and scalability in multi-agent learning. Among these, MAPPO \cite{yu2021surprising}, a multi-agent extension of PPO \cite{schulman2017proximal}, has become a state-of-the-art baseline for cooperative MARL. Building upon this, HAPPO and HATRPO \cite{kuba2021trust, zhong2023heterogeneousagent} introduced sequential agent-wise updates to further stabilize learning, achieving superior performance across various benchmarks.

However, these methods typically share the same global advantage value across agents (e.g., \( A_i = A_{tot}(s, a) \)), which can result in suboptimal updates. This is primarily due to the synchronous nature of policy updates, where shared credit fails to distinguish individual contributions and may hinder cooperation. Such issues are often attributed to the Relative Overgeneralization (RO) problem. To mitigate this, several approaches have explored more refined credit assignment techniques. Value-based methods like VDN \cite{sunehag2017value} and QMIX \cite{rashid2018qmix}, QTRAN \cite{son2019qtran}, QPLEX \cite{wang2020qplex} and policy-gradient methods like LICA \cite{zhou2020learning}, COMA \cite{foerster2018counterfactual}, VDAC \cite{su2021value}, and FACMAC \cite{peng2021facmac} assign credit from an individual perspective and have improved coordination efficiency \cite{DBLP:conf/icml/WangZHWZGHLF22, wang2020rode}. Additionally, DOP \cite{wang2020dop} has tackled the exploration challenge from a maximum entropy perspective.

Despite their success, these methods focus exclusively on either global or individual perspectives. Between these extremes lies an underexplored middle ground: coalitional granularity, where credits are evaluated and allocated at the level of agent subsets (i.e., coalitions \( C \subseteq N \)). To address this gap, recent works have introduced Shapley value-based credit assignment from cooperative game theory into policy gradient methods \cite{wang2020shapley, li2021shapley, wang2022shaq}. While these approaches provide theoretically grounded individual attributions, they often lack interpretability in the context of multi-agent policy updates and rely on rigid baselines (e.g., no-op or zero actions), which reduce flexibility. Furthermore, many other meaningful cooperative game solutions remain unexplored in MARL.

In this paper, we propose \textbf{Coalitional Rational Advantage Decomposition (CORA)}, a novel credit assignment framework for multi-agent policy gradient methods. CORA estimates \emph{coalitional advantages} by evaluating the marginal contributions of coalitions to the global return and decomposes credit using the \emph{core solution} from cooperative game theory. This ensures \emph{coalitional rationality} and preserves beneficial exploratory behaviors. To improve scalability, CORA employs \emph{random coalition sampling} for efficient approximation.

The main contributions of this paper are threefold:
\begin{itemize}
	\item We analyze the credit assignment problem from a coalition-level perspective and identify its limitations in existing global/individual methods.
	\item We propose CORA, a principled advantage decomposition method for policy gradients, using the $\epsilon$-core from cooperative game theory to ensure credit consistency and fairness across coalitions.
	\item We validate CORA across diverse multi-agent settings and demonstrate significant improvements over state-of-the-art credit assignment baselines.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related work}
\label{Sec:Related work}
This section provides an overview of key research areas relevant to our work, including traditional value decomposition methods, policy gradient methods.

\subsection{Value Decomposition Methods}
Value decomposition methods aim to decompose the global value function in MARL into individual contributions from each agent, thereby facilitating decentralized learning. Value-Decomposition Networks (VDN) \cite{sunehag2017value} is a pioneering approach that splits the joint action-value function into simpler, agent-specific value functions. This decomposition significantly reduces the complexity of multi-agent learning and allows for decentralized execution. QMIX \cite{rashid2018qmix}, an extension of VDN, introduces a monotonic mixing function that ensures the global Q-value is a monotonic combination of individual agent Q-values.

\subsection{Multi-Agent Policy Gradient Methods}
Policy gradient methods, particularly MAPPO (Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization) \cite{yu2021surprising}, have become the dominant paradigm in MARL. MAPPO adapts the well-known PPO algorithm to multi-agent environments by using a centralized critic and decentralized actors. This allows for improved sample efficiency and stability in complex tasks, where agents must learn to cooperate while maintaining decentralized control. MAPPO has shown significant performance improvements over earlier methods, such as COMA (Counterfactual Multi-Agent Policy Gradients) \cite{foerster2018counterfactual} and MADDPG (Multi-Agent Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient) \cite{lowe2017multi}, across various benchmarks like SMAC (StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge). The key challenge for MAPPO and similar methods is their difficulty in handling high-risk exploration in cooperative environments, where deviations from the global optimum can lead to significant losses. This interference problem impedes convergence to the global optimum and limits the exploration efficiency.

\subsection{Credit Assignment based on Shapley value}
Shapley-based methods in MARL, integrating cooperative game theory, address the credit assignment problem by fairly distributing rewards based on each agent’s contribution. Early work, such as SQDDPG \cite{wang2020shapley}, uses the Shapley value in Q-learning and DDPG for continuous action spaces to calculate each agent’s marginal contribution.

A more recent advancement, Shapley Counterfactual Credit Assignment (SCCA) \cite{li2021shapley}, refines credit assignment by considering counterfactual scenarios, improving accuracy and stability. However, SCCA faces computational challenges in multi-agent settings. SHAQ-learning \cite{wang2022shaq} also integrates Shapley values into Q-learning, enhancing stability and fairness in cooperative tasks, but it struggles with scalability and efficiency.

\subsection{Cooperative Game Theory and the Core}
Cooperative game theory, traditionally used in economics \cite{driessen2013cooperative}, is also applied in MARL for credit assignment. Recent works \cite{jia2019towards}, \cite{ghorbani2019data}, and \cite{sim2020collaborative} have adopted the Shapley value for data valuation and reward allocation. In federated learning, \cite{ray2022fairness} and \cite{donahue2021optimality} applied cooperative game theory to fairness and stability.

The core \cite{driessen2013cooperative}, another key concept in cooperative game theory, guarantees stability by ensuring no coalition of agents can improve their outcome by deviating from the allocation. While the Shapley value has been used for fair reward distribution in MARL, traditional methods often rely on a fixed baseline, limiting their applicability in dynamic environments. Additionally, they do not address interference from high-risk explorations in cooperative MARL.

\section{Background}
\label{Sec:Background}
This section provides an overview of the foundational concepts and challenges in MARL, focusing on policy gradient methods and the credit assignment problem.

\subsection{Multi-Agent Policy Gradient}

In cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning, a group of agents works together to maximize a shared cumulative reward within a common environment \cite{panait2005cooperative}. This setting can be formalized as a Markov game \cite{littman1994markov} defined by the tuple $\mathcal{G} = \langle N, S, A, \mathbb{P}, R, \gamma \rangle$, where $N = \{1, \dots, n\}$ is the set of agents, $S = S_1 \times \dots \times S_n$ is the joint or global state space, with $S_i$ being the state space of agent $i$, $A = A_1 \times \dots \times A_n$ is the joint action space, with $A_i$ being the action space of agent $i$, $\mathbb{P}: S \times A \rightarrow S$ is the state transition function, mapping a state and joint action to a distribution over the next states, $R: S \times A \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is the shared global reward function, $\gamma \in [0,1)$ is the discount factor.

%At each time step $t$, agents observe the global state $s^t = (s_1^t, \dots, s_n^t) \in S$, and each agent $i$ selects an action $a_i^t \in A_i$ according to its policy $\pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | s_i^t)$, where $s_i^t$ is the local state and $\phi_i$ are the policy parameters. The joint action $a^t = (a_1^t, \dots, a_n^t)$ leads to the next state $s^{t+1} \sim \mathbb{P}(\cdot | s^t, a^t)$ and a shared reward $r^t = R(s^t, a^t)$. The agents learn decentralized policies that maximize the expected cumulative reward:
%\begin{equation}
%	\max_{\pi} J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{s, a \sim \pi, \mathbb{P}} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t r(s_t,a_t) \right].
%\end{equation}

Under the centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) paradigm \cite{oliehoek2008optimal,lowe2017multi,yu2021surprising}, agents are trained using global information but execute using only local observations. A central component in training is the global state-action value function $Q_{tot}(s, a)$, estimating the expected return from state $s$ after taking joint action $a$. This function can be updated using temporal-difference (TD) learning \cite{sutton1998introduction}:
\begin{equation}\label{TD error}
	Q_{tot}(s^t, a^t) \leftarrow R(s^t,a^t) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{a^{t+1} \sim \pi} \left[ Q_{tot}(s^{t+1}, a^{t+1}) \right].
\end{equation}

The policy gradient for agent $i$ is defined by:
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:policy gradient}
	\nabla_{\phi_i} J(\phi_i) = \mathbb{E}_{s^t, a_i^t} \left[ \nabla_{\phi_i} \log \pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | o_i^t) A_i(s^t, a^t) \right].
\end{equation}

%The advantage value \( A_{tot}(s^t, a^t) = Q_{tot}(s^t, a^t) - b(s^t) \) is used to encourage the agent to update its policy, where \( b(s^t) \) is a baseline function that is independent of the action \( a^t \). The purpose of \( b(s^t) \) is to reduce variance in the estimation of the advantage, helping the agent to more efficiently update its policy.

%To solve the multi-agent credit assignment problem, COMA \cite{foerster2018counterfactual} introduces a counterfactual advantage function, which helps assign credit to individual agents by isolating the impact of their actions on the global reward. For agent \(i\), the counterfactual advantage is defined as:
%\begin{equation}
%	\label{eq:coma_advantage}
%	A_i(s^t, a_i^t) = Q_{tot}(s^t, a^t) - \sum_{a_i'} \pi_{\phi_i}(a_i' | o_i^t) Q_{tot}(s^t, (a_i', a_{-i}^t)),
%\end{equation}
%where \(a_{-i}^t\) denotes the actions of other agents. This function compares the global Q-value with the expected Q-value when agent \(i\)'s action is replaced, isolating the effect of \(i\)'s action on the global reward. By using this counterfactual baseline, COMA effectively assigns credit to individual agents, improving learning efficiency and coordination. 
% It reduces variance in the policy gradient estimation and helps agents learn policies that contribute positively to the team's performance.

For the credit assignment of policy gradient methods, the design of the advantage function $A_i$ plays a crucial role, aiming to provide fairer incentives for individual agents and to promote effective coordination.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Sharing the global advantage $A_i = A_{tot}(s, a) = Q(s, a) - V(s)$ among agents often leads to suboptimal policy updates. This method incentivizes each agent to update its policy $\pi_i(a_i|s_i)$ to either approach action $a_i$ with $A_{tot} > 0$ or avoid those with $A_{tot} < 0$. Many credit assignment methods such as COMA \cite{foerster2018counterfactual}, VDN \cite{sunehag2017value}, QMIX \cite{rashid2018qmix}, and LICA \cite{zhou2020learning} allocate advantage from an individual perspective. In addition to the global and individual levels, there exists an intermediate granularity, coalitions $C \subset N$ (i.e., subsets of the agent set $N$). In the following, we will revisit the multi-agent policy update process from the coalition perspective.

For example, when an action $a$ with $Q(s, a) \ll V(s)$ is explored during training, all agents are penalized via $A_{tot}(s, a) < 0$, and the policy $\pi_i(a_i|s_i)$ for each agent is updated to reduce its probability. This occurs even if a coalition $C \subseteq N$ could form a superior joint action $(a_C, \bar{a}_{N\setminus C})$ satisfying $Q(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N\setminus C}) > V(s)$. Moreover, consider the case where the executed action $a^*$ is already optimal. If agents in coalition $C$ explore a new action $a_C$ while others act optimally, and $Q(s, a_C, a_{N\setminus C}^*) \ll V(s)$, then the probability $\pi_i(a_i^*|s_i)$ for each agent $i \notin C$ is reduced due to $A_{tot}(s, a) < 0$, destabilizing the probability distribution over the optimal action $a^*$.

Additionally, given a baseline action $\bar{a}_i \in A_i$, during policy updates, agents with greater potential (those belonging to a coalition $C$ where $Q(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) \ll V(s)$) should receive larger advantage values to promote the joint action $(a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$. This encourages the emergence of promising coalitional behaviors by amplifying credit for advantageous deviations from the baseline action profile.

To be more specific, we will explain the problem through the following examples. Given state $s$ and sampled action $a = (a_1, \dots, a_n) \neq \bar{a}$, we consider Example \ref{tab:example1} and Example \ref{tab:example2} to discuss policy gradient update process.

\begin{table}[htbp]
	\centering
	\begin{minipage}{0.45\textwidth}
		\centering
		\caption{Example 1: Q-values for the joint actions}
		\label{tab:example1}
		\begin{tabular}{ccc}
			\toprule
			$A_{tot}(s, \cdot, \cdot)$ & $\bar{a}_1$ & $a_1$ \\
			\midrule
			$\bar{a}_2$ & 1 & -5 \\
			$a_2$       & -5 & 5 \\
			\bottomrule
		\end{tabular}
	\end{minipage}
	\hfill
	\begin{minipage}{0.45\textwidth}
		\centering
		\caption{Example 2: Q-values for the joint actions}
		\label{tab:example2}
		\begin{tabular}{ccc}
			\toprule
			$A_{tot}(s, \cdot, \cdot)$ & $\bar{a}_1$ & $a_1$ \\
			\midrule
			$\bar{a}_2$ & 1 & 5 \\
			$a_2$       & -5 & -5 \\
			\bottomrule
		\end{tabular}
	\end{minipage}
\end{table}

In Example 1, the globally optimal action is the sampled action $a$, with the highest advantage value. If sharing advantage methods are used, adopting the global advantage $A_{tot}(s, a) = Q_{tot}(s, a) - V(s) = 5$ for policy gradient updates, agents 1 and 2 will increase the probability of choosing actions $a_1$ and $a_2$ in state $s$ by a magnitude of 5, thus promoting the team to choose the globally optimal action.

In Example 2, sharing advantage methods face problems. Here, the globally optimal action is the combination $(a_1, \bar{a}_2)$, with the highest advantage value $A_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2) = Q_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2) - V(s) = 5$. If we directly use the global advantage $A_{tot}(s, a_1, a_2) = -5$ for policy gradient updates, agents 1 and 2 will decrease the probability of choosing actions $a_1$ and $a_2$ in state $s$ by a magnitude of 5, which prevents the team from selecting the globally optimal action $(a_1, \bar{a}_2)$. This means that sharing advantage collectively penalizes all agents, even though agent 1 and a baseline agent 2 could achieve a higher reward through their coordination.

Here, $A_{tot}$ is learned from the agents' past experiences. Since $A_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2) = 5 \geq A_{tot}(s, a) = -5$, an intuitive idea is that the credit $A_1(s_1, a_1)$ assigned to agent 1 during the policy update should not be too small. Specifically, we require $ A_1(s_1, a_1) \geq A_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2) - \epsilon$, where $\epsilon \geq 0$ should be minimized as much as possible.

\begin{remark}
	According to the estimation from the value network (actually, previous experience), the action combination $(a_1, \bar{a}_2)$ has potential for exploration in future. If there are significant estimation errors in $Q_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2)$ compared to $Q_{tot}(s, a_1, a_2)$, these errors can be reduced through subsequent interaction experiences, such as $(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2, r, s')$. Conversely, if the estimation error of $Q_{tot}(s, a_1, \bar{a}_2)$ is small, it suggests that $(a_1, \bar{a}_2)$ is indeed the globally optimal action, and thus, the probability of selecting $a_1$ should not be decreased.
\end{remark}

In the case of multiple agents, it is direct to extend the individual perspective to consider coalitions of agents. All possible $2^n$ combinations of the baseline action $\bar{a} = (\bar{a}_1, \dots, \bar{a}_n)$ and the sampled action $a = (a_1, \dots, a_n)$ should be considered, where each combination can be represented as a coalition action $(a_C, \bar{a}_{N \backslash C})$ limited in a coalition $C \subseteq N$ of agents. Similarly, the value of each coalition $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \backslash C}) = Q_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \backslash C}) - V(s)$ will serve to find the optimal action.

Given the computational complexity resulting from the exponential growth in the number of coalition combinations ($2^n$), practical implementations should employ approximation methods to reduce computational overhead.

\section{Coalitional Rational Advantage Decomposition for Multi-Agent Policy Gradients}
In this section, we introduce the advantage of coalition actions in subsection \ref{Coalitional Advantage} and propose a credits allocation algorithm in subsection \ref{Credits Allocation}.

\subsection{Coalitional Advantage}\label{Coalitional Advantage}

Consider a global value function $Q_{tot}(s, a)$, which describes the return of the joint action $a$ in state $s$. The advantage of coalition $C$, denoted as $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$, is defined as:
\begin{equation}
	A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) = Q_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) - V(s, \bar{a}).
\end{equation}

The first term in the above equation, $Q_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$, represents the global value when coalition $C$ executes sampled action $a_C$, and the other agents $N \setminus C$ execute the baseline action $\bar{a}_{N \setminus C}$. The coalitional advantage $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ evaluates the marginal contribution to global value when coalition $C$ tries new action $a_C$ sampled from their policies while others continue to execute the baseline action $\bar{a}_{N \setminus C}$.

\begin{remark}\label{baseline}
	During actual execution or evaluation of the agents, either $\bar{a_i}=\mu_{\theta_i} (s_i)$ or $\bar{a_i} = \arg\max_{a_i} \pi_{\theta_i}(a_i|s_i)$ is adopted. The former corresponds to continuous policies, e.g., Gaussian policy networks output $\mu_{\theta_i}$ and $\sigma_{\theta_i}$ to form a normal distribution $N(\mu_i, \sigma_i)$, then actions $a_i \sim N(\mu_i, \sigma_i)$ are sampled during training. While the latter corresponds to discrete policies, e.g., a probability distribution $\pi_i(\cdot|s_i)$ over the action set, and actions $a_i \sim \pi_i(\cdot|s_i)$ are sampled during training.

	We also use such actions as baseline actions in the credit evaluation and assignment process, representing the agent's unupdated/old action. In this way, we evaluate the coalitional advantage $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a_{N \setminus C}}) = Q(s, a_C, \bar{a_{N \setminus C}}) - V(s)$ (or when using only a Q-network: $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a_{N \setminus C}}) = Q(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) - Q(s, \bar{a}_C, \bar{a}_N)$), which represents the advantage gained when only the agents in coalition $C$ use new actions $a_i$, while other agents keep their original actions $\bar{a}_i$.
\end{remark}

% The second term, $Q_{tot}(s, \bar{a})$, represents the global value when all agents execute the baseline action $\bar{a}$. The coalitional advantage $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ evaluates the potential improvement in global value when coalition $C$ tries new action $a_C$ sampled from their policies instead of the baseline action $\bar{a}_C$, while others continue to execute the baseline action $\bar{a}_{N \setminus C}$.

% This setup has the ability to balance exploitation and exploration. The first term, $Q_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$, explores the potential gains or losses when coalition $C$ tries new actions sampled from their policies, i.e. $\pi_i(a_i|s_i)$. The second term, $Q_{tot}(s, \bar{a})$, provides a stable baseline for measuring the relative improvement of the coalition strategy.

%With this formulation, if $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) > 0$, it indicates that the coalition action $(a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ limited in coalition $C$ is better than the baseline action, making it worthy of further exploration and learning. 

By defining the advantage in this way, we can clearly quantify the contribution of each coalition action $a_C$ to the team.

\subsection{Credits Allocation}\label{Credits Allocation}

The next problem we need to solve is how to allocate credit $A_i(s_i, a_i)$ to each agent $i \in N$ based on $2^n$ advantage values $A_{tot}(s,a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ (for each $C \subseteq N$). Inspired by the discussion for Example \ref{tab:example2}, considering a coalition action $a_C$ has a high advantage value $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a_{N \setminus C}})$, the total advantage assigned to the agents in that coalition should not be too small, e.g.,
\begin{equation}\label{coalitional rationality eps}
	\sum_{i\in C}A_i \geq A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) - \epsilon.
\end{equation}

This form coincides with the classic solution Core of cooperative game theory \cite{driessen2013cooperative}. Thus, we use the Core solution from cooperative game theory to allocate the $A_i$ for each agent. By the constraint condition as \ref{coalitional rationality eps}, if the advantage value of a coalition action $(a_C, a_{N \setminus C})$ is positive while $A_{tot}(s, a) < 0$ due to other agents' exploration $a_{N \setminus C}$, it is still possible to assign a relatively high advantage to each $a_i$ for $i \in C$, thereby preventing these agents from being unfairly penalized. If coalition actions $(a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ are promising, it is beneficial to incentivize each $a_i$ ($i \in C$) to adjust its policy distribution, thereby encouraging exploration of this action in the future. 

This allocation principle aligns with coalitional rationality in cooperative game theory. Additionally, it is essential to ensure that $\sum_{i\in N} A_i(s_i, a_i) = A_{tot}(s, a)$, a condition known as effectiveness in cooperative game theory. For convenience, given current state $s$ and action $a$, we denote the advantage value of agent $i$, $A_i(s_i, a_i)$, simply as $A_i$.	

In the research of cooperative game theory \cite{driessen2013cooperative}, the set of non-empty allocations that satisfy the coalition rationality and effectiveness is known as the Strong $\epsilon$-Core:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		\text{Core}_{\epsilon} (N, A_{tot}) = \Big\{(A_1,\cdots,A_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n \Big| \sum_{i\in N}A_i = A_{tot}(s,a),
		\sum_{i\in C} A_i \geq A_{tot}(s,a_C,\bar{a}_{N\backslash C}) - \epsilon, \forall C\subseteq N\Big\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon \geq 0$ is a non-negative parameter that allows for a small deviation from the strict coalition rationality condition.

Generally, the $\epsilon$-core may contain infinite solutions, but not all allocation solutions are effective. Even allocation solution that satisfy the coalition rationality constraint may result in significant disparities in the credibility values among agents. Considering an example with three agents: the maximum global advantage $A_{tot}(s, a) = 9$ is achieved only when all agents take the exploratory action $a = (a_1, a_2, a_3)$. For any other combination of actions, the global advantage $A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) = 0$. According to the constraints of the $\epsilon$-core, using linear programming to minimize the coalition rationality deviation $\epsilon$, one possible solution is: $\epsilon = 0$, $A_1 = 9$, $A_2 = 0$, $A_3 = 0$. All the advantage is assigned to agent 1, while the actions of the other agents are not effectively incentivized. To address this issue, we incorporate an additional objective to minimize the variance of the credits among agents, leading to the following quadratic programming problem:
\begin{equation}\label{quadratic programming problem}
	\begin{aligned}
		\underset{\epsilon \geq 0, A_1, \dots, A_n}{\text{minimize}} & \quad \epsilon + \sum_{i \in N} \left(A_i - \frac{A_{tot}(s, a)}{|N|} \right)^2, \\
		\text{subject to:} 
		& \sum_{i \in N} A_i = A_{tot}(s, a), \\
		& \sum_{i \in C} A_i \geq A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) - \epsilon, \forall C \subseteq N.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

This ensures a more balanced allocation under the coalition rationality constraint. A direct analysis for the proposed credit allocation method is proposed as Theorem \ref{thm1}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm1}
	The credits $A_i(s_i, a_i)$ derived from (\ref{quadratic programming problem}) can incentivize agents to choose actions $a$ that increase the global value $Q_{tot}(s, a)$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Approximation with Sampled Coalitions}
Solving the quadratic programming problem (\ref{quadratic programming problem}) requires $2^{|N|}$ inferences of the value network to obtain $Q_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C})$ for each coalition $C \subseteq N$. This may results in significant computational overhead for large-scale problems. To address this issue, our method randomly samples a relatively small number of coalitions $\mathcal{C} = \{C_1, C_2, \cdots, C_m\}$ and computes the desired solution satisfing the constraints of these coalitions, resulting in the quadratic programming \ref{approximate quadratic programming problem}. Theorem \ref{thm2} shows that its approximation error can be controlled by the sample size \( m \).
\begin{theorem}\label{thm2}
	Given a distribution $\mathcal{P}$ over $2^N$, and $\delta$, $\Delta>0$, solving the programming (\ref{approximate quadratic programming problem}) over $O((n+2+\log(1/\Delta))/\delta^2)$ coalitions sampled from $\mathcal{P}$ gives an allocation vector in the $\delta$-probable core with probability $1 - \Delta$.
\end{theorem}

