\section{Technical Appendices and Supplementary Material}
%\section{Theorems, Lemmas, and Proofs}

In this appendix, we provide detailed statements of the theorems, lemmas, and their corresponding proofs presented in the main text. These results are crucial for understanding the theoretical foundations of our proposed method, CORA, and demonstrate the validity of the credit allocation approach in the multi-agent reinforcement learning setting.

\subsection{Theorem and Proof}

The approximate quadratic programming problem mentioned in the main text is as follows.

\begin{align}\label{approximate quadratic programming problem}
	\underset{\epsilon \geq 0, A_1, \dots, A_n}{\text{minimize}} & \quad \epsilon + \sum_{i \in N} \left(A_i - \frac{A_{tot}(s, a)}{|N|} \right)^2, \notag \\
	\text{subject to:} 
	& \sum_{i \in N} A_i =  A_{tot}(s, a), \notag \\
	& \sum_{i \in C_k} A_i \geq A_{tot}(s, a_{C_k}, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C_k}) - \epsilon, \forall C_k \in \mathcal{C}.
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}\label{appendix_thm1}
	The credits \( A_i(s_i, a_i) \) derived from the core-based method can incentivize the agents to choose actions that increase the global value \( Q_{tot}(s, a) \).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	Given the current state \( s \), \( V(s) \) is constant and independent of the selected action \( a \). Therefore, we have:
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\partial Q_{tot}(s, a)}{\partial A_{tot}(s, a)} = \frac{A_{tot}(s, a) + V(s)}{A_{tot}(s, a)} = 1.
	\end{equation}

	Since \( A_{tot}(s, a) = \sum_{i \in N} A_i(s_i, a_i) \), it follows that:
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\partial A_{tot}(s, a)}{\partial A_i(s_i, a_i)} = 1.
	\end{equation}

	Thus, we can conclude that:
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\partial Q_{tot}(s, a)}{\partial A_i(s_i, a_i)} = \frac{\partial Q_{tot}(s, a)}{\partial A_{tot}(s, a)} \cdot \frac{\partial A_{tot}(s, a)}{\partial A_i(s_i, a_i)} = 1 > 0.
	\end{equation}

	Therefore, the monotonicity holds, which implies that the value of \( Q_{tot}(s, a) \) increases monotonically with \( A_i(s_i, a_i) \). Through policy gradient updates, agents can select actions \( a_i \) to increase \( A_i(s_i, a_i) \). Hence, by credit decomposition, agents can improve their policy to increase the global value \( Q_{tot}(s, a) \).
\end{proof}

\subsection{Theorem 2: Approximation with Sampled Coalitions}
\begin{theorem}\label{appendix_thm2}
	Given a distribution \( \mathcal{P} \) over \( 2^N \), and parameters \( \delta \), \( \Delta > 0 \), solving the programming (\ref{approximate quadratic programming problem}) over \( O\left(\frac{n + 2 + \log(1/\Delta)}{\delta^2}\right) \) coalitions sampled from \( \mathcal{P} \) gives an allocation vector in the \( \delta \)-probable core with probability \( 1 - \Delta \).
\end{theorem}

The proof of this theorem follows an approach inspired by \cite{yan2021if}, where the core allocation is approximated using sampled coalitions. The key idea is to leverage the properties of the VC-dimension of a function class to bound the probability of deviating from the true allocation in the core. To establish this result, we introduce the following two known lemmas, which play a crucial role in the proof.

Before proving Theorem 2, we first introduce a lemma regarding the VC-dimension of a function class, as this concept is essential to understanding the behavior of the classifier we employ in the proof.

\begin{lemma}\label{appendix_lemma1}
	Let \( \mathcal{F} \) be a function class from \( \mathcal{X} \) to \( \{-1, 1\} \), and let \( \mathcal{G} \) have VC-dimension \( d \). Then, with \( m = O \Big( \frac{d + \log\left( \frac{1}{\Delta} \right)}{\delta^2} \Big) \) i.i.d. samples \( \{ \text{x}^1, \dots, \text{x}^m \} \sim \mathcal{P} \), we have:
	\[
	\Big| \mathop{\text{Pr}}_{x \sim \mathcal{P}} [f(\text{x}) \neq \text{y}(\text{x})] - \frac{1}{m}\sum_{i = 1}^{m} \mathbb{1}_{f(\text{x}^i) \neq \text{y}(\text{x}^i)} \Big| \leq \delta,
	\]
	for all \( f \in \mathcal{F} \) and with probability \( 1 - \Delta \).
\end{lemma}

This lemma essentially states that if the VC-dimension of a function class is \( d \), then by taking a sufficient number of samples \( m \), the empirical error rate of a classifier \( f \) on those samples is close to the true error rate with high probability (i.e., \( 1 - \Delta \)).

In the context of the theorem, we use linear classifiers to represent the core allocation constraints. The following lemma establishes the VC-dimension of the class of linear classifiers we use.

\begin{lemma}\label{appendix_lemma2}
	The function class \( \mathcal{F}^{n} = \{\text{x} \mapsto \text{sign}(\text{w} \cdot \text{x}): \text{w} \in \mathbb{R}^n \} \) has VC-dimension \( n \).
\end{lemma}

This lemma states that the VC-dimension of the class of linear classifiers is equal to the dimension \( n \) of the input space, which is important for bounding the number of samples required to approximate the core allocation effectively.

Now, we combine the insights from the previous lemmas to prove Theorem 2. 
\begin{proof}
	Consider a coalition \( C \) sampled from the distribution \( \mathcal{P} \). We represent the coalition as a vector \( \text{z}^C = (z^C, -A_{tot}(s,a_C,\bar{a}_{N \setminus C}), 1) \), where \( z^C \in \{0,1\}^n \) is the indicator vector for the coalition and \( A_{tot}(s,a_C,\bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) \) is the total allocation for the agents not in \( C \).

	We define a linear classifier \( f \) based on parameters \( \text{w}^f = (A, 1, \epsilon) \), where \( \text{w}^f \in \mathbb{R}^{n+2} \). The classifier \( f(\text{z}^C) = \text{sign}(\text{w}^f \cdot \text{z}^C) \) is designed to capture the core allocation for each coalition \( C \).

	To ensure coalition rationality, we want the classifier \( f \) to satisfy \( f(\text{z}^C) = 1 \) for all coalitions \( C \subseteq N \). This ensures that the allocation is in the core for all coalitions. The class of such classifiers is:

	\[
	\mathcal{F} = \left\{ \text{z} \mapsto \text{sign}(\text{w} \cdot \text{z}): \text{w} = (A,1,\epsilon), A \in \mathbb{R}^n \right\}.
	\]

	This class of functions \( \mathcal{F} \) has VC-dimension at most \( n+2 \) by Lemma \ref{appendix_lemma2}. 

	Now, solving the quadratic programming problem on \( m \) samples of coalitions \( \{C_1, \cdots, C_m\} \) provides a solution \( (\hat{A}, \hat{\epsilon}) \), and the corresponding classifier \( \hat{f} \). For each sample coalition \( C_k \), we have \( \hat{f}(\text{z}^{C_k}) = 1 \).

	By applying Lemma \ref{appendix_lemma1}, with probability \( 1 - \Delta \), we obtain the following inequality:

	\[
	\mathop{\text{Pr}}\limits_{C \sim \mathcal{P}} \Big[\sum_{i \in C} \hat{A}_i - A_{tot}(s, a_C, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}) + \hat{\epsilon} \geq 0\Big] \geq 1 - \delta.
	\]

	This shows that the allocation vector generated by solving the quadratic programming problem over the sampled coalitions is within the \( \delta \)-probable core with high probability (i.e., with probability at least \( 1 - \Delta \)).

	Thus, Theorem 2 is proved.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Algorithm Pseudocode and Diagram}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora} and Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora-v} outline the implementation of CORA within a standard actor–critic training loop. At each update, a set of coalitions is sampled, and the corresponding coalitional advantages are estimated. A constrained quadratic program is then solved to assign individual credits, which are used to guide policy updates. This procedure ensures that policy gradients reflect coalition-level contributions, encouraging coordination exploration. As noted in Remark~\ref{baseline}, Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora} uses $Q_{tot}(s, \bar{a})$ instead of $V(s)$ as the baseline and relies on a single network. An alternative setup, which aligns more closely with the common actor–critic paradigm, is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora-v}, where $V(s)$ is used as the baseline and an additional $Q$-network is employed to evaluate coalition advantages. In practical evaluations, the difference between the two is minimal. However, based on empirical observations, using Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora} is effective for discrete tasks, while Algorithm~\ref{alg:cora-v} performs better for continuous tasks.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
	\caption{Coalitional Rational Advantage Decomposition (CORA)}
	\label{alg:cora}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\small
		\STATE \textbf{Initialize:} Central critic network $\theta$, actor network $\phi_i$ for each agent $i$
		\FOR{each training episode $e$}
		\STATE Initialize state $s^{0}$ and experience buffer
		\FOR{each step $t$}
		\STATE Sample action $a^{t}_{i}$ from $\pi_i(a_i|s_i^t;\phi_i)$ for each agent
		\STATE Execute the joint action $(a_1^t,\cdots,a_n^t)$
		\STATE Get reward $r^{t+1}$ and next state $s^{t+1}$
		\STATE Add data to experience buffer
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Collate episodes in buffer into a single batch
		\FOR{$t=1,\cdots,T$}
		\STATE Compute the target value:\\
		\hspace{2em} $y^{t}=r(s^t,a^t) + \gamma Q_{tot}(s^{t+1},a^{t+1})$
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Update critic $\theta$ with the TD error (\ref{TD error})
		\FOR{$t=1,\cdots,T$}
		\STATE // Compute credits for state $s^t$:
		\STATE Sample $m$ coalitions $\mathcal{C}=\{C_1, \cdots, C_m\} \in 2^N$
		\STATE Estimate coalitional advantage:
		\[
		A_{tot}(s^t,a_C^t,\bar{a}_{N\setminus C}^t)=Q_{tot}(s^t,a_C^t,\bar{a}_{N\setminus C}^t)-Q_{tot}(s^t,\bar{a}^t)
		\]
		for each coalition $C \in \mathcal{C}$
		\STATE Solve the following programming (\ref{approximate quadratic programming problem}) to obtain the credits allocation solution $\hat{A}_i^t$
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Update actor networks $\phi_i$ for each agent using policy gradient or PPO-clipped policy gradient:\\
		\hspace{-2em} $\nabla_{\phi_i} \log \pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | s_i^t) \cdot \text{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | s_i^t)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a_i^t | s_i^t)}, 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon\right) \cdot \hat{A}_i^t$
		\ENDFOR
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
	\caption{CORA with Core-based Credit Assignment (based on state value function $V(s)$) and state-action value function $Q(s,a)$}
	\label{alg:cora-v}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\small
		\STATE \textbf{Initialize:} Central critic network $\theta_V$, $\theta_Q$; actor network $\phi_i$ for each agent $i$
		\FOR{each training episode $e = 1, \dots, E$}
		\STATE Initialize state $s^0$ and experience buffer
		\FOR{each step $t$}
		\STATE Sample action $a_i^t$ from $\pi_i(a_i | s_i^t; \phi_i)$ for each agent
		\STATE Execute the joint action $(a_1^t, \dots, a_n^t)$
		\STATE Get reward $r^{t+1}$ and next state $s^{t+1}$
		\STATE Add data to experience buffer
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Collate episodes in buffer into a single batch
		\STATE Compute the target value: $y^t = r(s^t, a^t) + \gamma Q_{tot}(s^{t+1}; \theta_V)$
		\FOR{$t = 1, \dots, T$}
		\STATE Sample $m$ coalitions $\mathcal{C} = \{ C_1, \dots, C_m \} \subseteq 2^N$
		\FOR{each coalition $C \in \mathcal{C}$}
		\STATE Estimate coalitional advantage:
		\[
		A_{tot}(s^t, a_C^t, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}^t) = Q_{tot}(s^t, a_C^t, \bar{a}_{N \setminus C}^t; \theta_V) - Q_{tot}(s^t; \theta_V)
		\]
		for each coalition $C \in \mathcal{C}$
		\STATE where $\bar{a}_i = \arg\max_{a_i} \pi_i(a_i | s_i^t; \phi_i)$
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Estimate grand coalition $N$'s advantage $A_{tot}(s^t, a^t)$ with GAE estimator
		\STATE Solve the programming problem to obtain credit allocation $\hat{A}_i^t$
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE Update actor networks $\phi_i$ using PPO-clipped policy gradient:
		\[
		\nabla_{\phi_i} \log \pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | s_i^t) \cdot \text{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\phi_i}(a_i^t | s_i^t)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a_i^t | s_i^t)}, 1 - \epsilon, 1 + \epsilon\right) \cdot \hat{A}_i^t
		\]
		\STATE Update critic $\theta_V$ using TD error: \quad
		$\sum_t \left(Q_{tot}(s^t; \theta_V) - y^t\right)^2$
		\STATE Update critic $\theta_Q$ using error: \quad
		$\sum_t \left(Q_{tot}(s^t, a^t; \theta_Q) - y^t\right)^2$
		\ENDFOR
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{img/others/diag.pdf}
	\caption{The framework of CORA in Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning.}
	\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:framework}, the framework demonstrates the process of Coalitional Advantage Estimation and subsequent Credit Allocation in policy gradient methods.

