\subsection{Concept Alignment}
\label{sec:concept_alignment}
We evaluate whether individual latent dimensions consistently represent the same high-level concepts across different input streams. We emphasize that assigning single concept labels to neurons is inherently limited—even if individual dimensions are monosemantic (our goal with SAEs), they might encode features that don't correspond well to available dataset labels. However, this analysis at least provides a systematic framework for comparing concept consistency across different configurations of TopK and $\lambda$ values.

To assign concept labels for this comparison, a natural approach is to analyze the TopK samples that most activate each latent, then assign the most frequent class label among them as the latent's dominant concept. However, this strategy can fail to reflect true semantic alignment when different streams activate on closely related but non-identical classes.

For example, one of our latents consistently activates for both \textit{tiger} and \textit{leopard} across streams. In a flat label space, these would appear as mismatched concepts. Yet in the Open Images hierarchy, both share a common ancestor:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{Tiger} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Carnivore} $\rightarrow$  \texttt{Mammal} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Animal} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Entity}
  \item \texttt{Leopard} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Carnivore} $\rightarrow$  \texttt{Mammal} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Animal} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{Entity}
\end{itemize}

As a result, we evaluate concept alignment using the Open Images taxonomy hierarchy, collapsing labels to different semantic depths to capture alignment at various levels of granularity. The collapse operation maps each label to its ancestor at a specified depth, creating semantic buckets where fine-grained labels like "tiger" and "leopard" both map to "carnivore" at intermediate depths.

For each latent $\ell$ and stream $s$, we identify the top-50 test samples with the highest activation $z_\ell^s$ and count label occurrences at each hierarchy depth. We then measure concept alignment using generalized Jaccard similarity between the label distributions of each latent across all stream pairs:

$$J_d(\ell; s_i, s_j) = \frac{\sum_c \min(a_c, b_c)}{\sum_c \max(a_c, b_c)}$$

where $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ represent the label count vectors for streams $s_i$ and $s_j$ at hierarchy depth $d$. We report the mean Jaccard similarity averaged over all latent dimensions and stream pairs.

Table~\ref{tab:alignment_results} shows the results across different hierarchy depths. Global TopK with cross-loss ($\lambda=1$) consistently achieves the highest alignment scores across all depths, reaching 0.80 Jaccard similarity at the finest granularity (depth 5). In contrast, Local TopK with cross-loss achieves only 0.26, while removing cross-loss ($\lambda=0$) further degrades performance for both methods. The results demonstrate that both the Global TopK mechanism and cross-reconstruction loss are essential for achieving the highest concept alignment.

