\paragraph{Why depth-0 scores are not 1.0.}
At depth \( d = 0 \), all labels are collapsed into the root class \texttt{Entity}. However, alignment scores still fall short of 1.0. This is because the metrics are sensitive to activation magnitudes: even if two streams activate on the same latent for semantically equivalent samples, any difference in activation counts (e.g., 500 vs. 450) reduces the overlap-based score.

This magnitude sensitivity provides a nuanced middle ground between overly lenient top-class assignment (which ignores secondary activations) and overly harsh binary presence/absence comparisons (which treat any divergence equally). For instance, if one stream activates with counts (human: 40, cat: 9, car: 1) while another shows (human: 50, cat: 0, car: 0), simple top-class assignment would report perfect alignment since both are dominated by "human," while binary presence/absence would heavily penalize the mismatch ({human, cat, car} vs {human}). The generalized Jaccard metric appropriately rewards primary concept agreement while proportionally penalizing differences in secondary activation patterns.

