\begin{abstract}

Boolean symbolic reasoning for gate-level netlists is a critical step in verification, logic and datapath synthesis, and hardware security. Specifically, reasoning datapath and adder tree in bit-blasted Boolean networks is particularly crucial for verification and synthesis, and challenging.
% \note{Should we use carry-chain or adder tree structure?}
Conventional approaches either fail to accurately (exactly) identify the function blocks of the designs in gate-level netlist with structural hashing and symbolic propagation,
% \note{What is this structural hashing method?}
or their reasoning performance is highly sensitive to structure modifications caused by technology mapping or logic optimization.
% failed to provide substantial reasoning support for real-world applications.
% \note{functional means cut enumeration? not failed}
This paper introduces \textbf{BoolE}, an exact symbolic reasoning framework for Boolean netlists using equality saturation. 
%For rewriting rules, BoolE selectively prunes the full rulesets to balance scalability and performance. Additionally, BoolE effectively extracts multi-output full adder structures within the e-graph through a novel extraction algorithm.
BoolE optimizes scalability and performance by integrating domain-specific Boolean ruleset for term rewriting.
% ruleset pruning in e-graph rewriting,\note{we no longer prune, but should emphasize we are reconstructing the original netlist and thus finding more, so may modify this sentence.}
% incorporates a novel extraction algorithm to efficiently identify and capture multi-input multi-output high-level structures (e.g., full adder) in the reconstructed e-graph, enhancing its structural insight and computational efficiency.
We incorporate a novel extraction algorithm into BoolE to enhance its structural insight and computational efficiency, which adeptly identifies and captures multi-input, multi-output high-level structures (e.g., full adder) in the reconstructed e-graph.

Our experiments show that BoolE surpasses state-of-the-art symbolic reasoning baselines, including the conventional functional approach (ABC) 
% \note{In cut enumeration method we only say it is functional}
and machine learning-based method (Gamora). Specifically, we evaluated its performance on various multiplier architecture with different configurations. 
Our results show that BoolE identifies $3.53\times$ and $3.01\times$ more exact full adders than ABC in carry-save array and Booth-encoded multipliers, respectively.
% \note{We may also mention reconstructing most npn fas.}
Additionally, we integrated BoolE into multiplier formal verification tasks, where it significantly accelerates the performance of traditional formal verification tools using computer algebra, demonstrated over four orders of magnitude runtime improvements.

% Boolean symbolic reasoning for gate-level netlists is a critical step in fields such as verification, logic and datapath synthesis, and hardware security. However, conventional approaches are limited to synthesizing only the Negation-Permutation-Negation (NPN) classes and fail to identify potential full adders when the netlist structure changes, particularly after technology mapping. In this paper, we propose BoolE, an exact symbolic reasoning for boolean networks
% framework that leverages equality saturation. BoolE demonstrates superior symbolic reasoning performance compared to the widely used synthesis tool ABC.

% To evaluate BoolE, we tested its performance on various full adder structures generated through technology mapping, which introduces additional challenges to symbolic reasoning. Our results indicate that BoolE achieves $94.45\%$ and $86.32\%$ of the theoretical upper bound in reasoning performance for carry-save-array (CSA) and Booth-encoded multipliers, respectively, representing significant improvements over the ABC synthesis tool. Additionally, netlists generated by BoolE exhibit a verification runtime that is $2,825\times$ faster for 24-bit CSA multipliers. For larger benchmarks, ABC-generated netlists exceed the verification time limits for bitwidths greater than 24 bits, whereas BoolE successfully verifies multipliers with bitwidths up to 128 bits.





\end{abstract}


