At last, after a decade of anticipation, 2024 has brought the world to a total of three exascale supercomputers --- yet humanity’s thirst for computing power has never been stronger. Indeed, the pre-training of Meta’s LLaMA 3.1 405B model required $3.8\times10^{25}$ FLOPs (in half precision), utilizing 16K GPUs (NVIDIA H100), on Meta’s private AI supercomputer \cite{meta_llama3}. This is nearly five orders of magnitude more computationally demanding than the 2023 Gordon Bell award-winning simulation, which ran for 8.56 minutes on Frontier—the world’s fastest scientific supercomputer at that time—and accounted for $3.38\times10^{20}$ FLOPs (in double precision) \cite{SC23_gordon_bell}. This shift underscores how Artificial Intelligence (AI) has de facto surpassed traditional scientific computing in raw computational demand.

Computational and data center operational efficiency are paramount to societal, scientific, and economic progress. However, the growing complexity of HPC infrastructures poses significant challenges for their management and maintenance. To address these challenges, Operational Data Analytics (ODA) has been proposed \cite{ODA} to collect and store production telemetry from data centers and apply data-driven analytics and visualization to improve efficiency and management \cite{netti2021conceptual}. Today, ODA is a critical component in many modern data centers, handling vast amounts of telemetry data generated by millions of sensors across diverse architectures, including cooling infrastructures, power supply units, system racks, and compute nodes. Researchers have primarily focused on providing an application layer on top of the ODA-collected telemetry, such as dashboards and digital twins \cite{m100nature,ExaDigit_SC}, as well as training AI models for anomaly detection and prediction \cite{borghesiADAP,AksarTPDS_anomalies_at_runtime}. In addition, a substantial body of work addresses job scheduling strategies based on dynamic resource management techniques, such as malleable jobs, which enable runtime adaptation of resource allocation to improve system throughput, utilization, and user experience \cite{malleable_job_TPDS}. However, direct query access to ODA’s monitored data by its stakeholders (facility managers, user support, end users, and system administration teams) has so far been limited.

